{
  "response": "Let me analyze this React Toast component for accessibility design issues. I'll work through the full investigation protocol systematically.\n\nFirst, let me understand what we're looking at: a `BuggyToast` component that displays a message and auto-dismisses after 3 seconds. The expected behavior mentions screen reader announcements with alert priority, keyboard dismissibility, and visual indicators for toast type \u2014 but I need to check if the implementation actually delivers on these expectations.\n\nLet me go through each phase of the protocol:\n\n**Phase 0 - Consume Test Evidence**: No test evidence provided in this case. The user has given us component code only.\n\n**Phase 1 - Pre-commitment Predictions**: For a toast notification, I predict:\n1. Missing `role=\"alert\"` or `aria-live` \u2014 screen readers won't announce the message automatically\n2. No keyboard dismissibility (no Escape key handler, no close button)\n3. No visual indicator for toast type (success/error) as mentioned in expected behavior\n4. Focus management issues \u2014 when toast appears/disappears, focus isn't managed\n\n**Phase 2 - Semantic HTML Audit**: The component uses a plain `<div>` with no semantic meaning. A div is not an interactive element and doesn't convey any role to assistive technology by default. There's no landmark structure, no heading hierarchy (though for a toast this might be okay), no form elements involved here.\n\n**Phase 3 - ARIA Pattern Compliance Audit**: This should follow the WAI-ARIA APG \"Status Message\" or \"Alert\" pattern. Looking at the code:\n- No `role=\"alert\"` \u2014 the expected behavior says \"announced with alert priority\" but there's no role attribute\n- No `aria-live` region\n- The component is just a div with text content\n\n**Phase 4 - Focus Management Review**: \n- When toast appears, focus isn't moved to it (though for non-modal notifications this might be acceptable)\n- There's no way for keyboard users to dismiss the toast \u2014 no Escape handler, no close button\n- The auto-dismiss happens but there's no manual dismissal option\n\n**Phase 5 - State Communication Audit**:\n- No `aria-live` or `role=\"alert\"` means state changes (toast appearing) aren't communicated to AT\n- No visual indicator for type (success/error) \u2014 the expected behavior mentions this but it's not implemented\n- The toast just appears and disappears with no programmatic announcement\n\n**Phase 6 - Multi-Perspective Review**:\n- Screen reader: Won't hear the toast at all since there's no live region or alert role\n- Keyboard-only: Can't dismiss manually, no Escape key support\n- Low vision: Visual styling exists but type indicators missing\n- Cognitive: Auto-dismiss after 3 seconds might be too fast for some users to read\n\n**Phase 7 - Gap Analysis**: What's missing?\n- Missing `role=\"alert\"` or `aria-live` \u2014 toast won't be announced by screen readers\n- Missing keyboard dismissibility (Escape key handler)\n- Missing close button with proper labeling\n- Missing visual type indicator (success/error styling and ARIA)\n- Missing focus management considerations\n\n**Phase 8 - Realist Check**: These are real issues that would cause actual user impact. Screen reader users wouldn't know the toast appeared at all, which defeats the purpose of a notification.\n\nLet me now formulate my findings with proper evidence and citations.\n\nThe component is very short (about 25 lines), so file:line references will be:\n- Line 17: `<div className=\"toast-notification\">` \u2014 missing role/aria attributes\n- Lines 9-13: useEffect timer \u2014 no keyboard dismissibility, no Escape handler\n- The entire return block lacks ARIA\n\nLet me structure my findings properly.I'll review this Toast component for accessibility design issues using the full investigation protocol. Let me start by reading and analyzing the code carefully.\n\n## Phase 0 \u2014 Consume Test Evidence\nNo automated test results or keyboard interaction traces were provided with this fixture. I'll proceed with source-code-based analysis, noting where measurement would strengthen findings.\n\n## Phase 1 \u2014 Pre-commitment Predictions\nBefore deep investigation, my predictions for a toast notification component:\n\n1. **Missing `role=\"alert\"` / `aria-live`** \u2014 The expected behavior says \"announced with alert priority\" but the code is just a `<div>`. Screen readers won't announce it automatically without an ARIA live region or alert role.\n2. **No keyboard dismissibility** \u2014 Expected behavior mentions \"keyboard user can dismiss toast if needed,\" but there's no Escape key handler, no close button, and no focusable element.\n3. **Missing visual type indicator** \u2014 Expected behavior says \"visual indicator shows toast type (success, error)\" but the component has no `type` prop or conditional styling/ARIA to communicate this.\n4. **No focus management on appearance/disappearance** \u2014 When a non-modal notification appears, it shouldn't steal focus, but there's also no consideration of whether focus should be managed at all.\n\n## Phase 2 \u2014 Semantic HTML Audit\n\nThe component renders:\n```jsx\n<div className=\"toast-notification\">\n  {message}\n</div>\n```\n\n**Findings:**\n- **Plain `<div>` with no semantic role**: A div conveys zero semantics to assistive technology by default. It is not a landmark, not interactive, and carries no inherent meaning. For a notification that must be announced, this is the root problem.\n- **No heading structure**: While toasts typically don't need headings, if there's a title + message distinction (implied by \"toast type\"), it should use semantic hierarchy. Currently just raw text in a div.\n- **No interactive elements**: There's no close button (`<button>`), which means keyboard users have zero way to dismiss the toast before the timer expires.\n\n**Verdict on semantics: MAJOR \u2014 The component uses a non-semantic `<div>` where ARIA roles or native semantics are required for the notification pattern.**\n\n## Phase 3 \u2014 ARIA Pattern Compliance Audit\n\nThe WAI-ARIA APG defines two relevant patterns here:\n1. **Alert (Status Message) pattern** \u2014 For messages that require user awareness but not necessarily focus\n2. **Dialog (non-modal)** \u2014 If dismissibility and interaction are required\n\nLooking at the code, there is **no ARIA whatsoever**:\n- No `role=\"alert\"` \n- No `aria-live` attribute\n- No `aria-atomic`, `aria-relevant` on a live region\n- No `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby` for identification\n\n**Expected behavior claims:** \"Screen reader announces toast message immediately\" and \"Message is announced with alert priority.\" Neither is achievable without ARIA. Per the WAI-ARIA APG Alert pattern, an element with `role=\"alert\"` is treated as a live region with assertive politeness \u2014 it interrupts the current announcement to speak the new content. Without this role (or `aria-live=\"assertive\"`), screen readers will not announce the toast at all unless focus happens to land on it.\n\n**Verdict: CRITICAL \u2014 The component fails the WAI-ARIA APG Alert pattern entirely. No ARIA attributes are present, so the notification is invisible to assistive technology.**\n\n## Phase 4 \u2014 Focus Management Review\n\nThe expected behavior states \"Keyboard user can dismiss toast if needed.\" Let me check what's implemented:\n\n```jsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n    const timer = setTimeout(() => {\n      setIsVisible(false);\n    }, duration);\n    return () => clearTimeout(timer);\n}, [duration]);\n```\n\n**Findings:**\n- **No Escape key handler**: Keyboard users cannot dismiss the toast early. The only way to remove it is waiting for the 3-second timer. This violates WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard) \u2014 if a keyboard user needs to dismiss content, they must be able to do so via keyboard.\n- **No focusable close button**: There's no `<button>` element in the toast at all. A proper toast should have a close/dismiss button that is focusable and activatable with Enter/Space.\n- **Focus on appearance**: When the toast appears, focus is not moved to it (which is actually correct for non-modal notifications \u2014 you don't want to steal focus from the user's current task). However, this means the only way AT users would know about the toast is through a live region announcement, which is missing.\n- **Focus on disappearance**: When `isVisible` becomes false and the component returns `null`, there's no focus restoration logic \u2014 but since focus was never moved to the toast, nothing needs restoring. This is acceptable for non-modal notifications.\n\n**Verdict: MAJOR \u2014 Keyboard users cannot dismiss the toast manually; no close button or Escape handler exists.**\n\n## Phase 5 \u2014 State Communication Audit\n\nThe expected behavior mentions several states that should be communicated:\n- \"Screen reader announces toast message immediately\" \u2192 **NOT implemented**. No `role=\"alert\"` or `aria-live`.\n- \"Message is announced with alert priority\" \u2192 **NOT implemented**. Without `role=\"alert\"`, the announcement won't have assertive priority.\n- \"Visual indicator shows toast type (success, error)\" \u2192 **NOT implemented**. There's no `type` prop, no conditional rendering of icons or ARIA attributes to indicate success vs. error.\n\n**Additional state communication gaps:**\n- No `aria-hidden` management: When the toast is hidden (`isVisible === false`), it returns `null`, so this isn't an issue. But if it were conditionally rendered with CSS display/visibility, `aria-hidden=\"true\"` would be needed on hidden instances.\n- No loading/error/success state distinction in ARIA \u2014 all toasts are identical from AT's perspective regardless of type.\n\n**Verdict: CRITICAL \u2014 Core notification state (appearance) is not communicated to assistive technology at all.**\n\n## Phase 6 \u2014 Multi-Perspective Review\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | HIGH | No `role=\"alert\"` or `aria-live` \u2014 toast is completely invisible to AT. User will never know it appeared unless they happen to be focused on the container. |\n| Keyboard-only | MEDIUM | Can't dismiss manually (no Escape, no close button). Tab order unaffected since nothing is focusable in the toast. |\n| Low vision | LOW-MEDIUM | Visual styling exists but type indicators missing. At 200% zoom, auto-dismiss may be too fast to read. No high contrast mode issues identified from code alone. |\n| Cognitive | MEDIUM | Auto-dismiss after 3 seconds may be too fast for users who need more time to process information. No pause/stop mechanism (WCAG 2.2.2). |\n| Vestibular & motion | LOW | No animations or transitions visible in the provided code. |\n| Auditory access | LOW | No media elements, no audio alerts. |\n| Environmental contrast | LOW-MEDIUM | Can't verify contrast ratios from code alone; depends on CSS not shown. Type indicators missing means color-only status indication is likely (WCAG 1.4.1). |\n\n**Detailed perspective notes:**\n\n- **Screen reader user**: The toast will be completely silent. No live region, no alert role. If the screen reader cursor happens to be in the area where the toast renders, it might read it during a refresh, but there's no guarantee of announcement. This is the most severe failure \u2014 the entire purpose of a notification (to inform the user) is defeated for AT users.\n\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Cannot dismiss the toast early. If the toast appears while the user is typing in a form field, they must either wait 3 seconds or tab around to find... nothing focusable. The expected behavior explicitly says \"keyboard user can dismiss toast if needed\" but this capability doesn't exist.\n\n- **Low vision user (200% zoom)**: At larger text sizes, the 3-second auto-dismiss may be too fast to read the full message. There's no mechanism to pause or extend the duration. WCAG 2.2.2 requires that moving/blinking content can be paused if it lasts more than a few seconds \u2014 though this is borderline since 3 seconds is short.\n\n- **Cognitive accessibility**: The auto-dismiss timer doesn't account for users who need more time. There's no option to extend or pause the notification. Error messages (if this toast type supports errors) should be persistent until dismissed, not auto-dismissing after 3 seconds. WCAG 2.2.2 and 7.2.1 considerations apply.\n\n## Phase 7 \u2014 Gap Analysis (What's Missing)\n\nExplicitly looking for what is ABSENT:\n\n1. **Missing `role=\"alert\"`** \u2014 The most critical gap. Without this, screen readers will not announce the toast. Per WAI-ARIA APG Alert pattern, `role=\"alert\"` causes immediate announcement with assertive priority. This is completely absent from line 17's `<div>`.\n\n2. **Missing keyboard dismissibility** \u2014 No Escape key handler anywhere in the component (lines 9-13 only handle the timer). Expected behavior says \"keyboard user can dismiss toast if needed\" but there's no mechanism for this. WCAG 2.1.1 requires all functionality to be available via keyboard.\n\n3. **Missing close button** \u2014 No `<button>` element exists in the component at all. A proper toast should have a visible, focusable, labeled close button (e.g., \"Dismiss notification\"). This is entirely absent from the JSX return block.\n\n4. **Missing `type` prop and type-based ARIA/visual indicators** \u2014 Expected behavior says \"visual indicator shows toast type (success, error)\" but there's no `type` parameter in the component signature (`{ message, duration = 3000 }`). No conditional rendering of icons, colors, or ARIA attributes based on notification type.\n\n5. **Missing `aria-live` fallback** \u2014 Even if `role=\"alert\"` were added, a robust implementation would also use `aria-live=\"assertive\"` with `aria-atomic=\"true\"` for broader AT compatibility. Neither is present.\n\n6. **Missing focus management consideration for persistent toasts** \u2014 If the toast type supports errors that should persist until dismissed (rather than auto-dismissing), there's no mechanism to keep them visible or manage focus appropriately.\n\n7. **CSS `visibility:hidden` / `display:none` on hidden content**: Not applicable here since the component returns `null`, but worth noting as a potential future gap if rendering strategy changes.\n\n8. **Missing `prefers-reduced-motion` consideration** \u2014 If CSS transitions are added later, they should respect reduced motion preferences (WCAG 2.3.3). Currently no animations exist in the code shown.\n\n## Phase 8 \u2014 Realist Check (Severity Calibration)\n\nLet me calibrate each finding:\n\n1. **CRITICAL: Missing `role=\"alert\"` / live region**\n   - Worst case: Screen reader user never knows a notification appeared. If it's an error message, they might think their action succeeded when it failed. This could lead to data loss or confusion.\n   - User group impacted: All screen reader users (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver)\n   - Detection speed: Would be caught in any screen reader user testing session immediately\n   - Severity is proportional \u2014 this completely defeats the purpose of a notification for AT users. **Keep as CRITICAL.**\n\n2. **MAJOR: No keyboard dismissibility**\n   - Worst case: Keyboard user must wait 3 seconds with no way to dismiss early. If multiple toasts stack, they accumulate without being clearable.\n   - User group impacted: All keyboard-only users\n   - Detection speed: Caught in first keyboard test\n   - Severity is proportional \u2014 the expected behavior explicitly requires this capability. **Keep as MAJOR.**\n\n3. **MAJOR: Missing type prop and visual/ARIA indicators**\n   - Worst case: Error toast looks identical to success toast visually, and AT can't distinguish them either. User might miss a critical error or be confused by a false success indication.\n   - User group impacted: All users, especially those with cognitive disabilities who rely on clear status indicators\n   - Detection speed: Caught in visual review or user testing\n   - Severity is proportional \u2014 type distinction is core to notification usability. **Keep as MAJOR.**\n\n4. **MINOR: No `aria-atomic` / `aria-relevant`** (if we were adding a live region)\n   - These are enhancement attributes that improve announcement quality but aren't strictly required if `role=\"alert\"` is used correctly.\n   - Would be an ENHANCEMENT, not a finding on its own since the base pattern isn't implemented yet.\n\n## Phase 9 \u2014 Self-Audit\n\nReviewing my findings:\n\n1. **Missing `role=\"alert\"`: HIGH confidence** \u2014 The code clearly shows no ARIA attributes on line 17's `<div>`. Developer cannot refute this with context. This is a genuine design gap, not preference. \u2713 Keep as CRITICAL.\n\n2. **No keyboard dismissibility: HIGH confidence** \u2014 Lines 9-13 show only a timer effect; there's no `keydown` handler for Escape anywhere in the component. The expected behavior explicitly requires this. Genuine gap. \u2713 Keep as MAJOR.\n\n3. **Missing type prop: HIGH confidence** \u2014 Component signature is `{ message, duration = 3000 }` with no `type` parameter. Expected behavior mentions visual indicators but they're not implemented. Genuine gap. \u2713 Keep as MAJOR.\n\n4. **No close button: HIGH confidence** \u2014 The JSX return block (lines 16-20) contains only the message text in a div, no `<button>`. Genuine gap. \u2713 Keep as part of keyboard dismissibility finding.\n\nAll findings have strong evidence and cite specific WCAG/APG references. No low-confidence items to move to Open Questions.\n\n## Phase 10 \u2014 Synthesis\n\nComparing against pre-commitment predictions:\n- \u2705 Predicted missing `role=\"alert\"` / live region \u2192 CONFIRMED (CRITICAL)\n- \u2705 Predicted no keyboard dismissibility \u2192 CONFIRMED (MAJOR)  \n- \u2705 Predicted missing visual type indicator \u2192 CONFIRMED (MAJOR)\n- \u2705 Predicted focus management issues \u2192 Partially confirmed \u2014 actually, the lack of focus stealing on appearance is correct for non-modal notifications. The real issue is that without a live region, there's no AT announcement at all.\n\nI did not miss anything I predicted. No surprises in either direction.\n\n---\n\n**VERDICT: REJECT**\n\nThe component fails to implement the most fundamental requirement of a toast notification: being announced to assistive technology users. The expected behavior explicitly requires screen reader announcements with alert priority, keyboard dismissibility, and type-based visual indicators \u2014 none of which are implemented. This is not an enhancement gap; it's a complete failure of the core accessibility design pattern for notifications.\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The `BuggyToast` component renders a plain `<div>` containing text that auto-hides after 3 seconds. It has no ARIA attributes, no live region or alert role, no keyboard dismissibility mechanism (no Escape handler, no close button), and no type-based visual or programmatic indicators. Screen reader users will never be notified when the toast appears \u2014 defeating its entire purpose as a notification. The component must be redesigned to implement the WAI-ARIA APG Alert pattern before it can ship.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**: All four predictions were confirmed:\n1. Missing `role=\"alert\"` / live region \u2192 CONFIRMED (CRITICAL)\n2. No keyboard dismissibility \u2192 CONFIRMED (MAJOR)\n3. Missing visual type indicator \u2192 CONFIRMED (MAJOR)\n4. Focus management issues \u2192 Partially confirmed \u2014 the real gap is that without a live region, there's no AT announcement at all\n\n---\n\n## Critical Findings (blocks access):\n\n1. **CRITICAL: Toast has no `role=\"alert\"` or `aria-live` \u2014 screen readers will never announce it**\n   - **Evidence**: Line 17: `<div className=\"toast-notification\">` contains only `{message}` with zero ARIA attributes\n   - **User group impacted**: All screen reader users (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver)\n   - **Expected behavior**: Expected behavior states \"Screen reader announces toast message immediately\" and \"Message is announced with alert priority.\" Neither is achievable without `role=\"alert\"` or `aria-live=\"assertive\"`. Per the WAI-ARIA APG Alert pattern, an element with `role=\"alert\"` is automatically treated as a live region with assertive politeness \u2014 it interrupts current speech to announce new content. Without this role (or equivalent), screen readers will not announce the toast unless focus happens to land on it.\n   - **WCAG citation**: WCAG 4.1.3 Status Messages \u2014 \"In content implemented using markup languages that support programmatic determination of status messages, status messages can be programmatically determined through an accessibility API.\" Also relates to 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (the div has no role).\n   - **Fix**: Add `role=\"alert\"` to the container `<div>`. For broader compatibility, also add `aria-live=\"assertive\"` and `aria-atomic=\"true\"`. This ensures immediate announcement with interrupt priority for all screen readers.\n\n2. **CRITICAL: No keyboard dismissibility \u2014 users cannot dismiss toast before auto-expiry**\n   - **Evidence**: Lines 9-13 show only a `setTimeout` timer effect; there is no `keydown` event handler, no Escape key support anywhere in the component (lines 1-25). The JSX return block (lines 16-20) contains no `<button>` or any focusable element.\n   - **User group impacted**: All keyboard-only users\n   - **Expected behavior**: Expected behavior explicitly states \"Keyboard user can dismiss toast if needed.\" This capability does not exist in the implementation. WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard) requires that all functionality be available via keyboard interface. If a notification appears and the user needs to clear it immediately (e.g., to see content behind it, or because multiple notifications are stacking), they have no way to do so without waiting for the timer.\n   - **WCAG citation**: WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard \u2014 \"All functionality of the content is operable through a keyboard interface.\" Also relates to 2.4.3 Focus Order and 2.1.2 No Keyboard Trap (if focus gets trapped in notification area).\n   - **Fix**: Add a close button (`<button>`) with an accessible label (e.g., `aria-label=\"Dismiss notification\"`), implement Escape key dismissal via a `keydown` handler on the toast container, and ensure the button is focusable and activatable with Enter/Space.\n\n---\n\n## Major Findings (significantly degrades experience):\n\n1. **MAJOR: Missing `type` prop \u2014 no visual or programmatic distinction between success/error/warning notifications**\n   - **Evidence**: Component signature on line 3: `const BuggyToast = ({ message, duration = 3000 })` has no `type` parameter. The JSX return (lines 16-20) renders only `{message}` with no conditional rendering of icons, colors, or ARIA attributes based on notification type.\n   - **User group impacted**: All users \u2014 especially those with cognitive disabilities who rely on clear status indicators; screen reader users who need to know whether a message is an error vs. success\n   - **Expected behavior**: Expected behavior states \"Visual indicator shows toast type (success, error, etc.)\" but this is not implemented at all. Without type distinction, an error notification looks identical to a success notification visually and programmatically. A screen reader user hearing the same announcement pattern for both errors and successes cannot distinguish critical failures from routine confirmations.\n   - **WCAG citation**: WCAG 1.4.1 Use of Color \u2014 \"Color is not used as the only visual means of conveying information.\" Also relates to 3.3.1 Error Identification (error messages should be identifiable) and 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions.\n   - **Fix**: Add a `type` prop (`'success' | 'error' | 'warning' | 'info'` with default). Render type-appropriate visual indicators (icons, color schemes) AND communicate the type programmatically \u2014 either via an icon with `aria-hidden=\"true\"` plus descriptive text in the message, or by including the type in the announced content. For error types specifically, consider using `role=\"alert\"` (already recommended above); for non-urgent informational toasts, use `role=\"status\"` instead.\n\n2. **MAJOR: Auto-dismiss timer has no pause/stop mechanism \u2014 may be too fast for users who need more time**\n   - **Evidence**: Lines 9-13 implement a hard-coded auto-dismiss with only the `duration` prop as configuration (default 3000ms). There is no hover/pause interaction, no \"extend\" button, and no way to prevent dismissal. The timer fires unconditionally via `setTimeout`.\n   - **User group impacted**: Users who need more time to read/process information \u2014 including those with cognitive disabilities, low vision users at high zoom levels, and screen reader users whose AT may take additional time to announce the content before it disappears\n   - **Expected behavior**: While not explicitly stated in expected behavior, WCAG 2.2.2 (Pause, Stop, Hide) requires that moving/blinking content lasting more than a few seconds can be paused or hidden by the user. A 3-second auto-dismiss is borderline \u2014 if screen reader announcement takes 1-2 seconds and the visual toast disappears at 3 seconds, users may miss critical information.\n   - **WCAG citation**: WCAG 2.2.2 Pause, Stop, Hide \u2014 \"For any information that automatically updates, moves, blinks, or scrolls... there is a mechanism for the user to pause, stop, hide, or control the timing.\" Also relates to 7.2.1 (timing adjustable) in some contexts.\n   - **Fix**: Implement hover/focus-based timer pausing (pause when mouse hovers over toast or keyboard focus enters it). For error-type notifications specifically, consider making them persistent until manually dismissed rather than auto-dismissing after a fixed duration.\n\n---\n\n## Minor Findings (friction but workaround exists):\n\n- **MINOR: No `aria-atomic` / `aria-relevant` on live region** \u2014 If implementing the recommended `role=\"alert\"`, these attributes are optional enhancements that improve announcement quality in some AT/browser combinations but aren't strictly required for basic functionality. Would be addressed as part of the CRITICAL fix above.\n\n- **MINOR: No `prefers-reduced-motion` consideration** \u2014 The current code has no animations or transitions, so this isn't an active issue. However, if CSS transitions are added later (common for toast fade-in/fade-out), they should respect `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)`. Flagged as a forward-looking note rather than an active finding since the provided code doesn't include motion.\n\n---\n\n## Enhancements (best practice not met):\n\n- **ENHANCEMENT: No skip-link or focus management for persistent toasts** \u2014 If error-type notifications are made persistent (as recommended in Major Finding #2), they should be placed at the top of the main content area and potentially receive focus if critical, so keyboard users encounter them immediately. Currently there's no landmark placement strategy.\n\n- **ENHANCEMENT: No `lang` attribute management** \u2014 If toast messages contain content in a language different from the page default, `lang` attributes should be applied to ensure correct pronunciation by screen readers. Not applicable for single-language apps but worth noting for internationalization.\n\n---\n\n## What's Missing (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions):\n\n- **Missing `role=\"alert\"` or `aria-live`** \u2014 The most critical gap. Without this, the toast is completely invisible to assistive technology. This isn't just an enhancement; it's a fundamental failure of the notification pattern itself.\n- **Missing keyboard dismissibility** \u2014 No Escape key handler, no close button. Expected behavior requires this but implementation provides none.\n- **Missing `type` prop and type-based ARIA/visual indicators** \u2014 Component signature has no way to distinguish success from error notifications. Both expected behavior and WCAG 1.4.1 require non-color-only status indication.\n- **Missing timer pause mechanism** \u2014 No hover/focus-based pausing of the auto-dismiss timer, which could cause users who need more time to miss critical information (WCAG 2.2.2).\n- **Missing close button with accessible label** \u2014 There is literally no `<button>` element in the component at all. A proper toast must have a focusable, labeled dismiss control.\n- **Missing `aria-atomic` / `aria-relevant`** \u2014 Enhancement attributes for live region announcement quality (would be addressed alongside the CRITICAL fix).\n- **Missing focus management strategy** \u2014 While not stealing focus on appearance is correct for non-modal notifications, there's no consideration of where toasts are placed in the DOM or whether they should be at the top of `<main>` for discoverability.\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes:\n\n- **Screen reader user**: The toast will be completely silent \u2014 no `role=\"alert\"`, no `aria-live` region, nothing to trigger an announcement. A screen reader user navigating a page where a form submission triggers this toast would have zero awareness that their action was processed (success or failure). This is the most severe accessibility failure in the component. If the toast contains error information, the user might think their submission succeeded when it actually failed \u2014 potentially leading to data loss or confusion about why expected outcomes didn't occur.\n\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Cannot dismiss the toast early. There's no close button (no `<button>` element exists), and there's no Escape key handler on the component or its container. If a keyboard user is interacting with form fields and a toast appears, they must either wait 3 seconds for it to disappear automatically or tab around looking for something focusable \u2014 finding nothing in the toast area. The expected behavior explicitly states \"keyboard user can dismiss toast if needed\" but this capability doesn't exist.\n\n- **Low vision user (200% zoom, high contrast)**: At 200% zoom, text reflows and the 3-second auto-dismiss may be too fast to read the full message before it disappears. There's no mechanism to pause or extend the timer on hover/focus. In Windows High Contrast mode, if type indicators rely solely on color (which they do \u2014 there are no visual icons or ARIA attributes), status information would be lost entirely. The component also lacks any `prefers-reduced-motion` handling for potential future animations.\n\n- **Cognitive accessibility**: The auto-dismiss timer doesn't account for users who need more time to process information. There's no option to pause, extend, or prevent dismissal of the notification. For error-type notifications specifically, WCAG 3.3.1 requires that errors be clearly identifiable and persist until addressed \u2014 an auto-dismissing error toast could cause a user to miss critical feedback about why their action failed. The lack of type distinction (success vs. error) means users can't quickly scan for important status changes.\n\n---\n\n## Verdict Justification:\n\n**REJECT** is warranted because the component fails its most fundamental accessibility requirement \u2014 being perceivable by assistive technology at all. A toast notification that screen readers cannot announce is not a notification; it's decorative content that happens to appear and disappear. The expected behavior explicitly requires AT announcements with alert priority, keyboard dismissibility, and type-based visual indicators \u2014 none of which are implemented.\n\nThis isn't an enhancement gap or a polish issue. It's a complete failure of the WAI-ARIA APG Alert pattern (or Status Message pattern). The component uses a plain `<div>` with no ARIA attributes whatsoever, meaning:\n1. Screen readers will not announce it (CRITICAL)\n2. Keyboard users cannot dismiss it manually (MAJOR)  \n3. No type distinction exists for any user group (MAJOR)\n\n**What would need to change for an upgrade:**\n- Add `role=\"alert\"` (or `aria-live=\"assertive\"`) with `aria-atomic=\"true\"` to the container \u2014 addresses CRITICAL #1\n- Add a focusable close `<button>` with Escape key handler and accessible label \u2014 addresses CRITICAL #2\n- Add a `type` prop with conditional visual/ARIA indicators for success/error/warning/info \u2014 addresses MAJOR #1\n- Implement timer pause on hover/focus, and make error-type toasts persistent until dismissed \u2014 addresses MAJOR #2\n\nOnce these four changes are implemented and verified (ideally with screen reader testing via virtual-screen-reader or a11y-test), the component should be re-reviewed for ACCEPT.\n\n---\n\n## Open Questions (unscored):\n\n- **CSS implementation**: The provided code only shows JSX/React logic, not the CSS for `.toast-notification`. Contrast ratios cannot be verified without seeing color values. If type indicators rely on color alone (likely given no icon/type prop exists), this would violate WCAG 1.4.1 even after a `type` prop is added \u2014 visual indicators must include non-color cues (icons, text, shape).\n\n- **DOM placement strategy**: Where in the DOM tree are toasts rendered? If they're appended at the end of `<body>` via portal, screen reader announcement behavior may differ from inline rendering. The WAI-ARIA APG recommends placing alert messages in a consistent location within the main content flow for predictable AT discovery.\n\n- **Multiple toast stacking**: How does this component behave when multiple toasts are triggered simultaneously? Each would need its own live region or they'd need to be grouped. Without seeing the parent/container implementation, it's unclear whether announcements would queue properly or overwrite each other.",
